The `default` case is executed when none of the specified cases in the `switch` statement match. This is particularly useful when `switch` fails to cover all the possible values, either because all the cases weren't specified or that the range of values was later expanded.

<!--more-->
### Bad practice
```cs
switch (node.Kind())
{
    case SyntaxKind.SimpleMemberAccessExpression: break;
    case SyntaxKind.NullLiteralExpression: break;
    case SyntaxKind.IdentifierName: break;
}
```
### Recommended
```cs
switch (node.Kind())
{
    case SyntaxKind.SimpleMemberAccessExpression: break;
    case SyntaxKind.NullLiteralExpression: break;
    case SyntaxKind.IdentifierName: break;
    default:
        throw new ArgumentException("Encountered unexpected node, i.e. `kind`");
        break;
}
```

